+# Research: Host Session Id on the End-of-Session Signal
+
+**Feature**: `001-bench` | **Date**: 2026-10-26 | **Plan**: [plan.md](plan.md) | **Spec**: [spec.md](spec.md)
+
+## Clarifications Resolved
+
+The Technical Context in [plan.md](plan.md) carried **no unresolved `NEEDS CLARIFICATION` items** — every field is determined by direct inspection of this checkout. The single non-technical unknown at run start (the project constitution) was resolved by a recorded substitution: **no `/memory/constitution.md` exists in this checkout; per the run instructions, the repository-root `AGENTS.md` served as the governance reference** (recorded in both the spec assumptions and the plan's Constitution Check).
+
+The questions below were the Phase 0 research tasks and their consolidated findings.
+
+## R-1: Where exactly is the end request built, and is it the only place?
+
+- **Decision**: The end-of-session POST is built at exactly one call site — the `request(f"{url}/v1/executions/{execution}/signal", ..., "POST", {"signal": signal}, {...})` call inside `_end_impl()` (`plugins/graphhelm/hooks/session_hook.py`, line 349). That is the single place to add the header.
+- **Rationale**: `_end_impl()` is the only function that calls `request(...)` with method `"POST"`; the sole `"GET"` call (briefing fetch) lives in `_start_impl()` on the start path, which FR-004 forbids touching. `request()` merges its `headers` argument over the built-in `Authorization` (and conditional `Content-Type`) headers, so adding one entry to that dict changes nothing else about wire format — `urllib.request.Request` receives the same URL, data bytes, and method.
+- **Alternatives considered**: A shared `end_headers(...)` helper (rejected — explicit task constraint: no new header helper; also a one-call-site abstraction is scaffolding Keel flags); a general-purpose header layer inside `request()` (rejected — would touch the shared path used by the start briefing GET, risking SC-003).
+
+## R-2: Which value is "the validated session id", and is it available and stable across retries?
+
+- **Decision**: Use the return value of `_session(payload)` — the same `str` already stored as `sessionId` in the delivery receipt and embedded in the actor id `{host}-session-{session}`. It is available at the end-POST site as the local variable `session` computed at the top of `_end_impl()`.
+- **Rationale**: `_session()` validates against the `ID` regex and cross-checks any configured `GRAPHHELM_SESSION_ID`, raising before any request on mismatch (lines 42–49), so a partial or guessed value can never reach the header. Because the actor string in the signal is derived from the very same value (FR-002 asks for "the same identifier already embedded in the actor identity"), header value correctness reduces to an identity check the code already performs. Retries re-run `_end_impl()`, re-validate the same input, and reuse the persisted receipt (`delivered == False` branch); the header therefore repeats with byte-identical value across attempts (FR-005) without any new state.
+- **Alternatives considered**: Reading the session id back from the stored delivery receipt rather than the local variable (equivalent value, one extra indirection — rejected for simplicity since the receipt is only created *after* validation anyway); parsing it out of the actor id string (rejected — loses the guarantee that the header matches pre-validation input).
+
+## R-3: How does the existing suite observe request headers, and which tests must be extended?
+
+- **Decision**: The in-process `RuntimeHandler.do_POST` already records every request as `(method, path, dict(headers), body)` in `RuntimeHandler.requests`; the contract test filters `POST` entries and reads `sent[0][2]`. Extending `test_end_posts_an_attributed_idempotent_observation_without_success_claim` with `self.assertEqual(sent[0][2]["X-GraphHelm-Actor-Session"], "session-123")` directly observes FR-001/FR-002 (missing header → `KeyError` failure; wrong value → assertion failure). Optionally, `test_failed_delivery_is_retryable_with_the_same_signal_bytes` should also compare the new header between `sent[0][2]` and `sent[1][2]`, mirroring its existing `Idempotency-Key` comparison and observing FR-005.
+- **Rationale**: This is the smallest observer that catches the plausible defects (header absent, wrong key spelling, value taken from the wrong source, dropped on retry) without weakening or restructuring any existing assertion (FR-006, SC-002). **Header casing is pinned by measurement, not assumption**: `urllib.request.Request` normalizes request header names before they hit the wire (measured on this checkout's interpreter, Python 3.11.9: a header built as `X-GraphHelm-Actor-Session` is captured server-side as `X-Graphhelm-Actor-Session`; the pre-existing `X-GraphHelm-Actor-Type` arrives the same way, which is exactly why line 186 already asserts `sent[0][2]["X-Graphhelm-Actor-Type"]`). The extended assertion must therefore read `sent[0][2]["X-Graphhelm-Actor-Session"] == "session-123"` — the same normalized spelling family the suite already uses — while the hook code itself spells the key `X-GraphHelm-Actor-Session`, consistent with its neighbors. HTTP header field names are case-insensitive (RFC 9110 §5.1), so the Runtime reads the identical field regardless of wire casing; the spec's contract name `X-GraphHelm-Actor-Session` is the canonical name recorded in the code and this artifact set.
+- **Alternatives considered**: A new dedicated test method for the header (rejected as redundant scaffolding — the spec names extending the *existing* end-request test, and folding assertions in keeps the contract test single-sourced); asserting via a separate header-capture fixture (rejected — the handler already captures headers verbatim).
+
+## R-4: Does anything else read these headers that this change could affect?
+
+- **Decision**: Within this checkout, only the hook's own test stand-in records request headers. The consuming Runtime (which reads `X-GraphHelm-Actor-Session` for structured presence attribution) is out of scope per the spec's assumptions, and an older Runtime ignoring the header completes delivery unchanged (SC-004), since `request()` validates only the JSON reply shape, never the request headers it sent.
+- **Rationale**: Grep-level inspection of `plugins/graphhelm/hooks/` shows header emission at the two `request()` call sites only; nothing in the hook reads response headers beyond status/reply size. Backward compatibility is by construction, not by configuration.
+- **Alternatives considered**: Adding a versioned feature-negotiation header pair (rejected — no evidence of consumers needing negotiation; pure overhead beyond scope).
+
+## Dependencies & Best Practices
+
+No new dependencies, storage formats, or integrations are introduced; consequently there are no best-practice or integration research tasks beyond R-1 through R-4 above. The only process constraint carried forward is AGENTS.md's English-only documentation rule, satisfied by all artifacts.
+
+**Output status**: all unknowns resolved; no `NEEDS CLARIFICATION` remains for Phase 1.
