+**Status**: Draft
+
+**Input**: User description: "When a GraphHelm-bound session ends, `plugins/graphhelm/hooks/session_hook.py` posts an `agent_session_ended` signal to the Runtime. The request names the actor (`X-GraphHelm-Actor`) but not the host session, so the Runtime's structured presence record has no session id. Send the host session id in the `X-GraphHelm-Actor-Session` header of that end request, and extend the existing end-request test in `plugins/graphhelm/hooks/test_session_hook.py` to assert it. Do not add a new header helper or change the start request; the hook's other tests must stay green."
+
+## User Scenarios & Testing *(mandatory)*
+
+### User Story 1 - Attribute the end-of-session observation to its host session (Priority: P1)
+
+A GraphHelm-bound agent session posts a structured "session ended" observation to the Runtime when it ends. Today the request identifies only the composite actor (host plus session) and carries no raw host session id, so the Runtime's structured presence record has nothing to link the observation to the specific host session that produced it. After this change, the end request sends the host session id in a dedicated request header next to the existing actor identity, making every end-of-session observation attributable to its originating session.
+
+**Why this priority**: This is the entire value of the feature. Without the session id on the end request, the Runtime cannot complete presence attribution for an ended session, and every downstream consumer inherits the gap.
+
+**Independent Test**: Can be fully tested by ending one bound session against a stand-in Runtime and inspecting the end request: the dedicated session header must be present with the exact session id, while everything else in the request (actor identity, signal body, idempotency key) is unchanged.
+
+**Acceptance Scenarios**:
+
+1. **Given** a session hook bound to an active execution, **When** the session ends and the observation is posted, **Then** the request carries the host session id in the dedicated session header with a value equal to the validated session id.
+2. **Given** the end request already names its actor, **When** the new header is added, **Then** the existing actor header remains present and unchanged and the request body is unchanged.
+3. **Given** a previously refused delivery of the same observation, **When** the retry is sent, **Then** the session header is present again with the same value as on the first attempt.
+
+---
+
+### User Story 2 - Guard the new contract with the existing end-request test (Priority: P2)
+
+The hook ships with a contract test that inspects the end request end-to-end. That test is extended so that a missing or wrong session header becomes a detected regression instead of a silent gap: the test asserts the header is present with the correct value, while every other hook test stays green, demonstrating that nothing else in the hook changed.
+
+**Why this priority**: It makes the attribution contract continuously enforced rather than manually checked. It depends on the P1 behavior but is independently verifiable by running the hook's test suite.
+
+**Independent Test**: Can be fully tested by running the hook's test suite and confirming the extended end-request test checks the session header's presence and value and passes, with all remaining tests passing unchanged.
+
+**Acceptance Scenarios**:
+
+1. **Given** the existing end-request contract test, **When** the suite runs, **Then** the test verifies the session header's presence and value and passes.
+2. **Given** the rest of the hook's test suite, **When** it runs after the change, **Then** every other test passes without modification.
+
+---
+
+### Edge Cases
+
+- **Invalid or mismatched session identity**: the hook validates the session id from its input before posting anything and already refuses without any request when a binding declares a different session or none is given. The change must not alter that behavior; a partial or guessed session header is never sent.
+- **Retry after refusal**: the session header value must be stable across delivery attempts so the Runtime sees identical attribution for one observation, no matter how many times it is sent.
+- **Start-of-session requests**: unchanged. They do not gain the session header, keeping the change scoped to the end request.
+- **Runtime that ignores the header**: delivery completes exactly as before (same acknowledgment, same delivered marking), so older Runtimes keep working and the change is additive.
+
+## Requirements *(mandatory)*
+
+### Functional Requirements
+
+- **FR-001**: System MUST send the host session id in the end-of-session signal request, in the dedicated `X-GraphHelm-Actor-Session` header, on every such request.
+- **FR-002**: The header value MUST exactly match the validated session id taken from the session hook's input — the same identifier already embedded in the actor identity.
+- **FR-003**: System MUST NOT change the end request's method, endpoint, signal body, idempotency key, or any pre-existing header.
+- **FR-004**: System MUST NOT modify the start-of-session request in any way.
+- **FR-005**: The session header MUST be present on every delivery attempt of an observation, including retries after a refused delivery.
+- **FR-006**: The existing end-request contract test MUST be extended to assert the session header's presence and correct value, and the hook's remaining test suite MUST stay green with no existing assertion weakened.
+
+### Key Entities
+
+- **End-of-session observation request**: The request the host hook sends to the Runtime when a bound session ends. Carries the typed "agent session ended" signal, the actor identity, the idempotency key, and — with this feature — the host session id header.
+- **Host session**: The session in which the agent runs, identified by the session id the hook receives in the end event and already validates against any declared binding.
+- **Presence attribution (Runtime side)**: The outcome this change enables — a structured record linking the ended-session observation to its host session. The Runtime's own handling of the header is out of scope for this feature.
+
+## Success Criteria *(mandatory)*
+
+### Measurable Outcomes
+
+- **SC-001**: 100% of end requests sent by a bound hook carry the session header with the correct session id, on first sends and retries alike (observed via the extended contract test).
+- **SC-002**: The hook's full test suite passes with the extended end-request assertion, with zero regressions among previously passing tests.
+- **SC-003**: Start-of-session requests are byte-for-byte unaffected: zero new or changed headers on the start path (verified by needing no changes to any start-path test).
+- **SC-004**: End-to-end delivery completes identically with the header present: the observation is acknowledged and marked delivered in the same number of requests as before, confirming the change is purely additive and backward compatible.
+
+## Assumptions
+
+- The hook input always provides the session id in the end payload; the hook already validates it and refuses mismatches before any request, so discovery and validation of the session id are unchanged.
+- The session header is request metadata only: it does not enter the signal body, does not affect the idempotency key, and requires no change to any persisted state format.
+- No new helper abstraction is introduced for building headers; the header is added at the single place where the end request is built, per the task constraint.
+- The Runtime can consume the header independently of this change; no Runtime change, deployment, or schema update is required and is out of scope.
+- Other host integrations that reuse this same hook inherit the header automatically and need no separate change.
+- All documentation in this repository is written in English per the repository documentation-language rule.
+- Governance substitution recorded per run instructions: no `/memory/constitution.md` exists in this checkout; the repository-root `AGENTS.md` was used as the governance reference instead.
